1. Executive Summary
PayTune is a YouTube-inspired, pay-per-view (TVOD) video and audio marketplace tailored for the Rwandan market and its diaspora. Unlike traditional streaming platforms that rely on recurring monthly subscriptions, PayTune operates on a "Pay Once, Watch Forever" model.

The platform empowers local artists to monetize their original content (music videos, audio tracks, and short-form videos), while providing fans with a seamless, secure, and high-quality playback experience. The system handles complex financial escrow, dual-currency pricing, and automated revenue splitting between the creator and the platform.

2. Core Business Logic & Monetization
A. The "Pay Once, Watch Forever" Rule
When a user buys a video, the system creates a permanent Entitlement (Purchase record).

The user can watch the video unlimited times, on any device, forever.

No subscriptions. No rent-expiry timers (like 48-hour rentals). Once bought, it is owned.

B. Revenue Splitting & Escrow
When a user pays (e.g., 1,000 RWF), the money goes into the Owner's Escrow Account first. The backend instantly calculates the split:

5% VAT: Deducted for tax compliance.

Remaining 95% is split:

70% goes to the Artist's Wallet (e.g., 665 RWF).

30% goes to the Platform Owner's Wallet (e.g., 285 RWF).

Note: All financial calculations are done in the backend and logged with unique transaction IDs.

C. Dual Currency Pricing
Local Users (Rwanda): Detected via IP. Prices are displayed in RWF. Payments are made via MTN MoMo or Airtel Money.

Diaspora Users (Global): Detected via IP. Prices are displayed in USD. Payments are made via Credit Card (Stripe).

Artists must set both an RWF price and a USD price for every paid video.

D. The Token & PIN Payment Flow
User clicks "Buy" and enters their phone number.

The backend generates a Unique Transaction Token (e.g., PT-7K4M-9X2B).

This token is displayed on the user's screen.

The user receives a USSD push on their phone. They must verify the token matches the screen, then enter their MoMo PIN.

The provider sends a Webhook to PayTune. The backend verifies the amount, marks the payment as Paid, grants access, and splits the funds.

